-- §22 What each fusion declared it cannot see: the offsets between its own parts.
# One row per unordered pair of parts inside one fusion: the rate at which one part's supply
# stands in for the other's, and the sentence the composer wrote to license it.
#
# What the rate means
#   A fusion adds its parts after converting each by its own factor, so taking some of part A's
#   own supply out and the matching amount of part B's in, at the ratio of A's factor to B's,
#   leaves the composed figure exactly where it was. That ratio is the exchange rate, in the parts'
#   own units.
#
# A report, and it must never become a rule
#   `asrt:Fusion` says the judgement that the parts stand in for each other is the composer's, and
#   that no validator reaches it: whether a unit of supply in one part can serve demand in the
#   other is a claim about the world, not about the arithmetic. So this relation carries no verdict
#   and accuses nobody. What it does is put a size beside the claim, because `asrt:Fusion/observed`
#   asks for the judgement to be written where a reader can disagree with it, and a reader
#   disagreeing needs to know how much is being asserted rather than only that something is.
#
# What getting it wrong costs
#   `asrt:Fusion` names the damage of fusing what does not stand in for each other: a group
#   remainder smaller than the real one, because one member's excess covered another's shortfall
#   across a boundary nothing crosses. That sentence is about this rate, and this measures it.
#
# A one-part fusion has no row, and that is the whole of its claim
#   With one part there is no sibling to stand in for, and the fusion asserts nothing of the kind.
#   Its absence here is the finding, not a gap.
#
# The row count is not the number of independent offsets
#   This relation returns one row per pair, so a fusion of three parts gives three rows while only
#   two of them are independent: the third offset is the other two taken together. They agree for
#   one part and for two. No fusion here has more than two parts, so the totals agree today for a
#   reason that is a fact about the corpus, not about the arithmetic. `rank/composition_kernel.sqlc`
#   counts the independent offsets and this relation does not; summing these rows to get that count
#   would be right today and wrong at the first fusion with three parts.
#   `eliminations/derived.sqlc` pairs the parts the same way and carries the same caveat, which is
#   why neither relation's row count may be read as a correction.
#
# A blank factor leaves the rate open, and says so
#   `asrt:Part/factor` may be filed as a typed blank, and then the rate is not a number anybody can
#   write. The pair still exists and the fusion still added them, so the row stays, `determined` is
#   false, and `undetermined_because` names the part whose factor is missing. A blank rate and a
#   rate of one are different facts, and merging them would report a fusion as asserting an
#   exchange at par when nobody said so.
#
# Opposite ends of the two factors
#   The two factors belong to different parts and are filed independently, so the rate takes its
#   low from A's lowest factor over B's highest, and its high from A's highest over B's lowest, with
#   nothing counted twice. A part with no factor at all converts at exactly one, a single value and
#   not a range, so it adds no width of its own.
#
# Unordered, and a part is not paired with itself
#   A part does not stand in for itself, and naming the pair twice would say the fusion declared
#   two offsets where it declared one. The row comparison keeps each pair once, as
#   `eliminations/derived.sqlc` does.
-- asrt:Fusion/asrt:Part crossed with itself on the fusion, carrying each part's factor.
WITH
sized AS (
    SELECT p.composition, p.composed_layer,
           p.part_filing, p.part_layer, p.factor_state,
           CASE WHEN p.factor_state = 'omitted' THEN 1 ELSE p.factor_low  END AS lo,
           CASE WHEN p.factor_state = 'omitted' THEN 1 ELSE p.factor_mode END AS md,
           CASE WHEN p.factor_state = 'omitted' THEN 1 ELSE p.factor_high END AS hi
    FROM ( :compose(composition/parts.sqlc) ) p
)
SELECT a.composition                                        AS "composition!",
       a.composed_layer                                     AS "composed_layer!",
       a.part_filing  || '/' || a.part_layer                AS "part_a!",
       b.part_filing  || '/' || b.part_layer                AS "part_b!",
       (a.lo IS NOT NULL AND b.lo IS NOT NULL)              AS "determined!",
       least(a.lo / b.hi, a.hi / b.lo)::double precision    AS rate_low,
       (a.md / b.md)::double precision                      AS rate_mode,
       greatest(a.lo / b.hi, a.hi / b.lo)::double precision AS rate_high,
       CASE WHEN a.lo IS NOT NULL AND b.lo IS NOT NULL THEN NULL
            ELSE concat_ws(' and ',
                     CASE WHEN a.lo IS NULL
                          THEN 'the factor on ' || a.part_layer || ' is ' || a.factor_state END,
                     CASE WHEN b.lo IS NULL
                          THEN 'the factor on ' || b.part_layer || ' is ' || b.factor_state END)
       END                                                  AS undetermined_because,
       f.observed                                           AS "observed!"
FROM      sized a
JOIN      sized b
       ON b.composition    = a.composition
      AND b.composed_layer = a.composed_layer
      AND (b.part_filing, b.part_layer) > (a.part_filing, a.part_layer)
JOIN      (
    :compose(composition/fusions.sqlc)
) f ON f.filing = a.composition AND f.layer = a.composed_layer
ORDER BY a.composition, a.composed_layer, "part_a!", "part_b!"
